::GEO_U04_L05_Q21::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L05_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which side is opposite the right angle? {
=A. The hypotenuse#The hypotenuse is always opposite the right angle.
~B. A leg#The legs form the right angle.
~C. The shorter side#The shorter side is usually a leg, not the side opposite the right angle.
~D. The adjacent side#Adjacent depends on a chosen acute angle.
}

::GEO_U04_L05_Q22::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L05_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation fits legs 14 and 48 with hypotenuse c? {
~A. 14 + 48 = c<sup>2</sup>#The legs must be squared before adding.
=B. 14<sup>2</sup> + 48<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>#The squares of the legs add to the square of the hypotenuse.
~C. 14c + 48 = 0#This does not represent the Pythagorean relationship.
~D. 14<sup>2</sup> + c<sup>2</sup> = 48<sup>2</sup>#This treats 48 as the hypotenuse instead of c.
}

::GEO_U04_L05_Q23::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L05_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which set of side lengths forms a right triangle? {
~A. 4, 5, 6#4<sup>2</sup> + 5<sup>2</sup> is not 6<sup>2</sup>.
~B. 5, 6, 7#5<sup>2</sup> + 6<sup>2</sup> is not 7<sup>2</sup>.
=C. 6, 8, 10#6<sup>2</sup> + 8<sup>2</sup> = 10<sup>2</sup>.
~D. 6, 7, 12#6<sup>2</sup> + 7<sup>2</sup> is not 12<sup>2</sup>.
}

::GEO_U04_L05_Q24::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L05_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>What is &radic;144? {
~A. 72#Dividing by 2 does not find a square root.
~B. 24#Doubling 12 is not the square root.
~C. &radic;12#&radic;144 simplifies completely to 12.
=D. 12#12 squared equals 144.
}

::GEO_U04_L05_Q25::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L05_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>What is the longest side of a right triangle called? {
=A. Hypotenuse#The hypotenuse is the longest side and is opposite the right angle.
~B. Leg#Legs form the right angle and are shorter than the hypotenuse.
~C. Median#A median is a segment from a vertex to a midpoint.
~D. Altitude#An altitude is a height segment.
}
